WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.430
Steve Kaplan: And so after Connections you
have the part of the journey called New Directions.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.190
And New Directions is when your discovered
goal happens.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:15.790
In a Hero’s Journey, the hero goes off to
right a wrong.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.699
And whether they right the wrong or not, that’s
the whole movie.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.490
Are they going to save the rebellion or not?

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:31.210
They might meet Yoda along the way but their
goal stays the same really.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:38.700
But in comedy, because your characters start
off as missing a part or broken, their initial

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.870
goals are broken as well.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:41.870
They're shortsighted.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:50.350
They are not really the best for the character
and the discovery goal is what the characters

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.690
discover along the way, what they really want.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.900
Bill Murray starts GROUNDHOG DAY thinking
all that would make his life perfect would

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.549
be to be a weatherman at a bigger, better
station.

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:10.490
But along the way he realizes what he really
wants is having a real relationship, and specifically,

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:14.780
having a real relationship with Rita (Andie
MacDowell’s character).

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:21.810
In TROPIC THUNDER the goal is simply to get
back to the base camp and get out of the jungle

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.539
where you might step on a landmine and blow
yourself up.

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:32.110
But along the way, they realize that their
fellow actor Ben Stiller has been captured

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:39.759
by these drug lords and so you have them have
a different goal now.

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:48.670
Is it simply “Do we just leave him here
and go back or do we try to save him, even

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.670
though we’re just actors?

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.889
We’re not action heroes.

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:53.959
We’re not really soldiers.”

00:01:53.959 --> 00:02:01.069
And that’s where another really important
part of comedy comes out, is decision points.

00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:08.479
You need real decision points in terms of
your comic hero’s journey.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:14.290
You want to avoid a situation where there
is no question of what your character is going

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.709
to do.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:19.599
It’s completely obvious the way it should
go.

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:23.730
Why even bother wasting my time?

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:32.010
One example of that in an otherwise well-received
movie, but one that I don’t think really

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:39.159
give its protagonist a great decision point,
is MIDNIGHT IN PARIS.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:47.620
Because in MIDNIGHT IN PARIS, Owen Wilson
had to choose between this whiny, harpy of

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:56.450
a girlfriend, who he is engaged to or one
of the women he has met in his time travels

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:00.189
in Paris of earlier years.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:02.220
To me, that’s not a real decision point.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:09.790
Do you really think he’s going to give up
the romance of Paris and go back to California

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.900
with this really annoying woman?

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:20.870
A better decision point is in another comedy
(SLEEPLESS IN SEATTLE) in which Meg Ryan is

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.689
engaged to Bill Pullman.

00:03:22.689 --> 00:03:26.150
Bill Pullman is a great guy!

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.750
But is he “They Guy?” and that’s a really
great decision point because you could support

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.969
her going either way.

00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:38.660
You could see it from her point of view going
either way.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.549
There is this “Maybe” waiting for her
at the top of the Empire State Building but

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:47.380
there is Bill Pullman right in front of you
being such a nice guy.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.989
So you want real decision points.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.290
And in SPY, you see Melissa McCarthy having
some real decision points.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.049
She has a new goal.

00:03:56.049 --> 00:04:01.940
Her goal is no longer to have Jude Law be
her boyfriend.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:10.230
This whole mission started out as a way of
kind of avenging him.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:20.160
She often hurts her adversary by saying “That’s
for Bradley.”

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.720
But eventually her goal changes.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.990
It isn’t just to avenge Bradley Fine.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:31.819
Her goal is to do what she is meant to do
which is “I can do this.

00:04:31.819 --> 00:04:33.030
I can be this spy.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.340
I don’t have to hide behind someone else.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.090
I can do this and I can save the world.”

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.950
And save the world from having bad guys get
this destructive device.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:53.360
And so you see this moment in which she’s
been given these instructions: File your report.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.039
Do nothing else.

00:04:55.039 --> 00:05:01.180
And there is a bad guy who is running away
from her that is obviously the best lead to

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:06.460
get to where the destructive device is hidden.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.440
And you see it in that moment.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:18.500
Do I follow orders or do I follow my own emerging,
transforming sense of self and do what I think

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:19.840
is right?

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:26.000
So at that moment we see her discover a new
goal for herself.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.910
Her new goal is no matter what, she is going
to see this through to the end.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:30.259
Question: What are your thoughts on a hero
having a second goal or a “discovered goal?”

